/*! @arch_page arch-disagg-roles Disaggregated Storage Roles and Role Changes

Disaggregated storage allows multiple WiredTiger nodes to read shared data while a single node
persists it to shared storage. A connection's role determines its access to shared storage, the
view it reads, and the work it performs on behalf of the cluster. Changing that role affects
the whole storage engine, not only individual tables or cursors.

This page describes how WiredTiger implements roles and role changes. For the application
contract, including configuration, call sequences, and restrictions, see
@ref disagg_role_management.

@section disagg-roles-steady-state Leader and follower

The <b>leader</b> is the only node that writes to shared storage: it writes the stable shared
tables, publishes schema changes to shared metadata, and produces the checkpoints that other
nodes read. A <b>follower</b> reads a checkpointed view of those tables and applies recent changes
locally, without writing them back to shared storage. A follower can create and drop tables
locally, but only a leader publishes those changes to other nodes.

For application data, @ref arch-disagg-layered "a layered table" combines a shared <b>stable
table</b> with a node-local, in-memory <b>ingest table</b>. On a leader, the stable table is a live
writable btree and the ingest table is empty. On a follower, the stable table is a read-only
checkpoint view, with ingest table changes layered over it. The two constituents allow
checkpoint adoption without writing follower changes into the shared btree.

Followers eventually adopt new checkpoints produced by the leader. Adopting a checkpoint makes
covered ingest table content eligible for removal once it is safe for readers. See
@ref arch-disagg-layered-cursor for the merged view.

@section disagg-roles-coordination Role changes

The application changes a connection's role through WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure. <b>Step-up</b>
changes a follower into a leader, and <b>step-down</b> changes a leader into a follower. The two
transitions are asymmetric: step-up incorporates the follower's local changes into the shared
tables it starts writing, while step-down switches to a read-only view of a checkpoint and
retains any later changes in the ingest tables. The diagram summarizes both roles and the
transitions between them.

@image html disagg-roles.svg

@section disagg-roles-effects Effects across WiredTiger

Because the role applies to all shared state on the connection, each role change updates the
following responsibilities together:

| Area | Effect of the role |
|------|--------------------|
| Shared checkpoints | The leader writes the shared checkpoint; followers adopt it as a new base for reads. |
| Shared metadata | The leader publishes schema changes to shared metadata during checkpointing; followers adopt the published schema with the checkpoint. |
| Shared history store | The leader writes historical versions to shared storage. Followers read the history associated with their checkpoint. |
| Encryption key-provider metadata | The leader persists encryption key-provider metadata with shared checkpoints; followers read the published metadata. |
| Shared table views | Step-up switches from checkpoint views to live writable tables; step-down switches to read-only checkpoint views. |
| Eviction | A leader can reconcile shared pages. A follower reclaims covered ingest table content without writing it back to shared storage. |
| Shared disk cache | A follower caches shared page images locally. Step-up makes the cache read-only until it is retired; step-down re-enables it. |
| Background work | Checkpoint cleanup is leader-only work; the deferred checkpoint pickup server runs on followers. Step-up and step-down stop or restart these services. |

See @ref arch-disagg-layered for layered tables, @ref arch-hs for historical versions,
@ref arch-disagg-key-provider for encryption key metadata, and @ref arch-eviction for page
reclamation.

@section disagg-roles-step-up Step-up

Before step-up, a follower already has a shared checkpoint view and recent changes in its
ingest tables. Step-up first adopts any checkpoint whose pickup was deferred, so the new leader
continues the shared checkpoint lineage from the newest base rather than forking it from an
older one. It then switches the role, restarts any in-progress checkpoint, creates the stable
tables missing for tables created on the follower, and drains the remaining ingest table changes
into the stable tables. After successful reconfiguration, the connection can perform normal
leader writes and produce shared checkpoints from that base.

Step-up does more than allow writes to the follower's existing view: it combines checkpointed
data and local changes into a consistent live shared tree. Draining moves ingest content from
under positioned cursors, and a snapshot taken on the follower cannot be consistent with the
drained and adopted stable tables, so transactions and positioned cursors must not span
step-up. See @ref disagg_role_management_step_up for the application requirements.

@section disagg-roles-step-down Step-down

Step-down transfers shared-write responsibility without restarting the old leader and changes
it to a follower. After the transition, the node reads its stable tables through read-only views
of the concluding checkpoint, so every change it must still serve has to be either in that
checkpoint or in its ingest tables. The application therefore needs a clear boundary between
writes that belong in the concluding checkpoint handed to the next leader and later writes that
must be stored locally for follower operations. There are two ways to establish it.

@subsection disagg-step-down-blocking Blocking step-down

The simplest boundary is to stop writing. In <b>blocking step-down</b>, writes stop while the
concluding checkpoint is taken and remain stopped through the reconfiguration. Until
reconfiguration, the node still operates as a leader and writes to its live stable tables.
Reconfiguration switches those tables to read-only checkpoint views, so updates newer than the
checkpoint would no longer be available through the follower's stable tables. The concluding
checkpoint's latency therefore becomes part of the period in which the application cannot write.

@subsection disagg-step-down-async Asynchronous step-down

<b>Asynchronous step-down</b> defines the boundary in advance so writes can continue while the
concluding checkpoint is taken. The application sets a step-down timestamp N, and transactions
that begin after it is set keep a copy of their writes in the ingest tables. The concluding
checkpoint is taken at N, so the node can switch to checkpointed stable table views without
losing newer writes. Writes must still stop for the step-down reconfiguration itself, so
asynchronous step-down shortens the period of write unavailability but does not eliminate it.

The rest of this section describes how writes are routed while N is set, why some transactions
are rolled back, how reads and schema operations behave, and what the reconfiguration does.

@subsection disagg-step-down-routing Write routing

WiredTiger chooses the destination of a transaction's writes using the step-down state recorded
at transaction begin. A transaction that began before N was set writes to the stable tables
only. A transaction that began after N was set writes to the ingest tables, and in the default
mode to the stable tables as well.

The <code>disaggregated=(stepdown_write_mirroring)</code> setting of ::wiredtiger_open selects
between the two modes for transactions that begin after N. For applications, the modes differ
mainly in the write-conflict detection they guarantee: mirrored writes detect every conflict at
the conflicting write, as outside a step-down, while ingest-only writes can miss a conflict with
a straddling writer until that writer is rolled back.

- <b>Mirrored writes (default):</b> each write to an existing layered table goes to the stable
  table first and then to the ingest table, within the same transaction. Because every writer,
  including those that began before N was set, writes to the stable table, WiredTiger's normal
  single-btree conflict detection handles conflicts between them. The stable table versions
  above N are excluded from the concluding checkpoint by its timestamp.
- <b>Ingest-only writes (legacy):</b> writes go to the ingest tables only. Writers on the two
  sides of the boundary no longer share an update chain, so each ingest-only write performs an
  explicit check against the stable table for conflicting updates. This check does not always
  detect conflicts immediately with writers that began before N was set and straddle setting
  the step-down timestamp. Those writers are rolled back rather than committed, so the missed
  conflicts cannot persist.

In both modes, the ingest table holds the copy that survives the step-down reconfiguration.
Mirroring trades additional stable table writes and reconciliation for immediate conflict
detection through a single btree; ingest-only writes avoid the extra copy but rely on the
cross-table check.

@subsection disagg-step-down-transactions Why straddling writers are rolled back

A transaction's commit timestamp may be set after some of its writes have already occurred, so
until the commit timestamp is known, WiredTiger cannot tell on which side of N those writes
belong. Rather than guess, it rolls back write transactions that straddle setting the step-down
timestamp. Such a writer has written to the stable tables
only: if it committed above N, its updates would be excluded from the concluding checkpoint and
would have no ingest table copy to survive the step-down reconfiguration, leading to data loss.

A layered-table write checks whether the transaction began before N was set and returns
WT_ROLLBACK when it observes the change, failing the transaction early. The commit path also
checks for straddling writers. A retried transaction begins with N set and writes to the ingest
tables.

@subsection disagg-step-down-reads Reads during step-down

Reads follow the same decision made at transaction begin. A transaction that began after N was
set reads the ingest table layered over the still-live stable table. One that
began earlier reads the stable table alone, which already holds everything its snapshot can
see. The decision stays correct only while the transaction keeps reading the state it saw at
begin. For that reason, layered-table operations must run in explicit snapshot-isolation
transactions while N is set, and WT_SESSION::reset_snapshot is not allowed.

The step-down reconfiguration replaces each live stable table with a view of the concluding
checkpoint. The checkpoint keeps older versions in the shared history store, which supports
reads at a timestamp, so a transaction with a read timestamp selects the same versions from the
checkpoint view. A transaction without a read timestamp that straddles reconfiguration may be
rolled back because its snapshot cannot be assumed to select consistent content from the
replacement.

@subsection disagg-step-down-schema Schema changes during step-down

A layered table created after N is set has the follower table shape: only its local ingest
table is created. Writes to such a table always go to the ingest table and are never mirrored,
so a stable table would stay empty, and the concluding checkpoint must not include the table.

Without schema epochs, the concluding checkpoint applies every other schema operation queued
when it starts, including drops issued after N was set. With schema epochs, the step-down epoch
divides schema operations the same way N divides data. See @ref disagg_role_management_schema
for the schema-epoch contract.

*/
